y, independence, homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors), and normality of residuals. If these assumptions are violated, the R² value may be misleading, as it could suggest a strong explanatory power when, in fact, the model may be poorly specified or the relationships may not be linear.\n\nThe importance of R² in econometrics lies in its ability to provide a quick assessment of model fit, guiding researchers in model selection and evaluation. However, it is crucial to complement R² with other diagnostic measures, such as adjusted R², which accounts for the number of predictors in the model, and various statistical tests that assess the validity of the model assumptions.\n\nIn summary, R² is a vital statistic in regression analysis that measures the explanatory power of the FLR variable. Its value is directly influenced by the inclusion of the FLR regressor, making it essential for researchers to consider the implications of variable selection on model performance. Understanding R² and its relationship with FLR enhances the ability to construct robust econometric models that accurately reflect the underlying data dynamics.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771166168, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 582, "prompt_tokens": 288, "total_tokens": 870, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0